/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  thermoPhysPropDict (v2) -- T6 dilute-solution (Henry) world.  Henry is a
  CONVENTION for the solutes group, not a model of the whole liquid: the
  solvent sits on the pure-liquid (Raoult) rung, each solute on the
  infinite-dilution rung.  The builder VERIFIES every declared pair file.
  Migrated mechanically from the v1 flat solvent/solutes form (physics
  identical -- the solutes list states the engine's own Henry selection);
  v2 contract 2026-07-17.

  ARGON is in the solutes group.  Without the Ar-NH3 pair, argon fell onto the
  Raoult rung with a vapour pressure extrapolated ABOVE its critical
  temperature: K_Ar = 1.7 in the separator against K_N2 = 124, and argon left
  dissolved in the product instead of accumulating in the loop.  The pair was
  fitted from the IUPAC-NIST Solubility Data Series Vol. 4 -- see the header
  of constant/parameters/Henry/Ar-NH3.dat and the dossier in curation/Ar-NH3/.

  ABOVE Tc (DEV.md 4c C256, measured 2026-11-09).  Every run announces
  `[psatContinued]` for H2, N2, Ar and NH3: a vapour pressure asked ABOVE the
  critical temperature (33, 126, 151 and 405 K), where no saturation state
  exists and the engine continues ln Psat linearly in 1/T.  The C256 ladder
  was applied and this package is already on its first rung: every
  supercritical gas that can dissolve (H2, N2, Ar) is a Henry solute with a
  measured pair, so NO K-value of this plant is a continued Psat.  Measured
  by scaling every continued Psat above Tc by 10 and by 0.1 (a scratch build,
  not committed): no stream, no unit duty, no balance, no size, no cost and
  no economic number moves -- the separator's dissolved gases and both
  let-down flashes are the Henry rung's.  A cubic phi-phi package (the
  ladder's second rung) is NOT taken: it would replace measured solubilities
  with an SRK whose H2-NH3 and N2-NH3 interaction parameters are zero, and
  it would not reach the one place the curve still shows (next sentence).
  WHAT STILL READS IT, an engine rule and not this package: the five
  phaseChanger coolers print `Tsat` by inverting the DOMINANT component's
  Psat -- hydrogen or nitrogen, far above Tc -- so their `Tsat` is the
  bisection's floor (150 K), and the latent / sensible split of the
  WaterCooler and the Chiller is priced at that temperature.  Their total
  duty is right; the split is not a physical number (x0.1 moves it by 160 %).
  The Gibbs converter's supersaturation check also compares y_i P with a
  continued Psat at 733 K; it passes because the continued value is large.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

recordType    thermophysicalPropertySystem;
schemaVersion 2;

components       ( N2  H2  NH3  Ar );

equilibrium
{
    formulation diluteSolution;

    liquid
    {
        solvent
        {
            component     NH3;
            standardState pureLiquid;          // the Raoult rung
        }
        solutes
        {
            components    ( N2 H2 Ar );
            standardState infiniteDilution;    // the Henry rung
            solutionModel henryDilute;
            binaryParameters
            {
                N2-NH3 { source "data/standards/parameters/Henry/N2-NH3.dat"; }
                H2-NH3 { source "data/standards/parameters/Henry/H2-NH3.dat"; }
                Ar-NH3 { source "constant/parameters/Henry/Ar-NH3.dat"; }
            }
        }
    }

    vapour
    {
        fugacityModel SRK;
    }
}

//  TRANSPORT (2026-10-07, DEV.md 4c C48).  No unit of this flowsheet reads a
//  transport property, so this block moves no number of the solve; it is here
//  because system/postDict DESIGNS the six exchangers in detail (Kern), and a
//  film coefficient needs the gas's viscosity and conductivity.  Chung (gas mu
//  from Tc, Pc, omega, M) and Eucken (gas k from Cp and mu), mixed by Wilke's
//  rule -- DILUTE-GAS models: at the loop's 150 bar the real gas is more
//  viscous and more conductive than this, and every exchanger sheet says so
//  in its basis.  Chung announces, per component, what it estimates.
transport
{
    vapour
    {
        viscosity           { model Chung; }    // gas mu   (Tc, Pc, omega, M)
        thermalConductivity { model Eucken; }   // gas k    (Cp + mu)
    }
}
